  "answer": "Covalent compounds have low melting points compared to ionic compounds because the forces of attraction between covalent molecules are weak. In covalent compounds, atoms are held together by sharing electrons, and the molecules are not arranged in a crystalline structure like ionic compounds. As a result, covalent compounds are usually gases or liquids at room temperature and have low melting and boiling points. In contrast, ionic compounds have strong electrostatic forces of attraction between oppositely charged ions, which require more energy to break, leading to high melting and boiling points.",
